.TH MKFS.MXFS 8 "2026-03-10" "MXFS 0.10.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mkfs.mxfs \- format a block device for MXFS (Multinode XFS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkfs.mxfs
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.I device
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfs.mxfs
creates an MXFS filesystem on
.IR device .
MXFS (Multinode XFS) is a shared/clustered filesystem that enables
concurrent multi-node read/write access to a single block device.
.PP
The tool creates the following on-disk layout:
.PP
.nf
  [MXFS super 4KB] [journal ~64MB] [disklock ~32MB] [XFS data]
.fi
.PP
The MXFS superblock is written at offset 0, preventing the device from
being accidentally mounted as a standard XFS filesystem.  The XFS data
region is formatted as a valid XFS v5 filesystem with CRC, ATTR2, FTYPE,
FINOBT, and LAZYSBCOUNT features enabled.
.PP
.B mkfs.mxfs
is a standalone program with no external dependencies.  It does not
require
.BR mkfs.xfs (8)
or any libmxfs linkage.  All XFS structures are written natively.
.SS On-Disk Regions
.TP
.B MXFS Superblock (4 KB)
Contains magic number (0x5346584D), format version, filesystem UUID,
and byte offsets to the journal, disklock, and XFS data regions.
Protected by CRC32C.
.TP
.B Journal (~64 MB)
Per-node circular write-ahead journals.  Contains a 512-byte journal
superblock followed by 64 journal slots of 1 MB each.  Each node claims
a slot on mount for crash recovery.
.TP
.B Disklock (~32 MB)
Heartbeat region (64 slots x 512 bytes = 32 KB) followed by 65536 lock
slots (32 MB) for the CAW (Compare-And-Write) disk-based DLM.
.TP
.B XFS Data (remainder)
Standard XFS v5 filesystem with 4096-byte blocks, 512-byte inodes, and
approximately 1 GB allocation groups.  The XFS region grows toward the
end of the device, allowing future expansion with
.BR resize_mxfs (8).
.SS Total Reserved Space
Approximately 96 MB is reserved for the MXFS superblock, journal, and
disklock regions.  The remaining space is available for XFS data.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f
Force formatting.  Skip the interactive confirmation prompt.
.TP
.BR \-v
Verbose output.  Display detailed information about each formatting
step, including per-slot journal initialization.
.TP
.BR \-V
Print the version string and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Format a shared iSCSI LUN for use with MXFS:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkfs.mxfs /dev/sdb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Format without confirmation prompt:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkfs.mxfs -f /dev/sdb
.fi
.RE
.PP
After formatting, mount the filesystem on each node:
.PP
.RS
.nf
insmod mxfs.ko
mount -t mxfs /dev/sdb /mnt/shared
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Filesystem created successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (device too small, I/O error, permission denied, etc.).
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 3
The device must be at least 200 MB.
.IP \(bu 3
The device is opened with
.B O_EXCL
to ensure it is not currently mounted.
.IP \(bu 3
A UUID is generated from
.I /dev/urandom
and written to both the MXFS superblock and the XFS superblock.
.IP \(bu 3
XFS geometry: 2 or more allocation groups (target ~1 GB each), 4096-byte
blocks, 512-byte sector size, 512-byte inodes, inode alignment of 8.
.IP \(bu 3
The root directory inode number is 128 (block-aligned at block 16 in AG 0).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/mkfs.mxfs
The format tool binary.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/fsck.mxfs
Symlink to
.BR chk_mxfs (8)
for fstab fsck dispatch.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mxfs (5),
.BR chk_mxfs (8),
.BR resize_mxfs (8),
.BR mount (8),
.BR mkfs.xfs (8)
